      content: '**Motor Fluctuation Assessment**:\n\n**Wearing-off phenomenon**: This patient is on carbidopa-levodopa TID. With disease progression, the duration of benefit from each dose shortens. Key questions:\n- How long does each dose last? (Typical early: 4-6 hours; wearing off: <4 hours)\n- Does he notice symptoms returning before the next dose?\n- Are there predictable "off" periods tied to medication timing?\n\n**Motor fluctuation types to identify**:\n1. **Wearing off**: Predictable return of symptoms before next dose — most common. This patient likely has this given H&Y Stage 3 after 6 years.\n2. **On-off phenomenon**: Unpredictable, sudden transitions between mobile ("on") and immobile ("off") states\n3. **Dose failure**: Occasional dose does not take effect — may relate to protein intake interfering with levodopa absorption\n4. **Peak-dose dyskinesias**: Involuntary choreiform movements at peak levodopa levels\n5. **Diphasic dyskinesias**: Occur at rising and falling levodopa levels\n\n**Medication diary**: Ask the patient and wife to complete a 3-day motor diary documenting "on," "off," "on with dyskinesia," and sleep times in 30-minute intervals.\n\n**Levodopa challenge test**: Administer 1.5x the usual morning dose after 12-hour medication washout. A >30% improvement in UPDRS-III confirms levodopa responsiveness — a prerequisite for DBS candidacy.\n\n**Deep Brain Stimulation (DBS) Candidacy Criteria**:\n1. **Idiopathic Parkinson disease** (not atypical parkinsonism — MSA, PSP, CBD)\n2. **Robust levodopa response** (>30% improvement on challenge test)\n3. **Motor fluctuations and/or dyskinesias** inadequately controlled with optimized medication\n4. **Disease duration >4 years** (to ensure correct diagnosis)\n5. **No significant cognitive impairment** — MoCA score of 22 is borderline. Formal neuropsychological testing is essential. DBS in patients with PD-MCI or dementia worsens cognitive outcomes.\n6. **No untreated psychiatric disease** (active psychosis, severe depression)\n7. **Realistic expectations** — DBS improves motor fluctuations, tremor, and dyskinesias but does NOT improve levodopa-unresponsive symptoms (freezing of gait in "on" state, postural instability, speech, cognitive decline)\n\n**For this patient**: His freezing of gait and postural instability are concerning because these are often **axial symptoms that may not respond to DBS**. The MoCA of 22 warrants formal neuropsychological evaluation before proceeding. He should be referred to a movement disorders center for multidisciplinary DBS evaluation.',
