      content: '**Evaluation of Visual Hallucinations in Parkinson Disease**:\n\nVisual hallucinations in PD are common (affecting 20-40% of patients) and represent a spectrum from benign to concerning:\n\n**Classification**:\n1. **Minor hallucinations (passage/presence)**: Sense of something passing in peripheral vision or feeling of a presence — common, usually benign\n2. **Formed visual hallucinations**: Seeing people, animals, or objects — as in this patient\n3. **Hallucinations with loss of insight**: Patient believes hallucinations are real — indicates PD psychosis\n\n**Differential Diagnosis**:\n1. **Medication-induced**: Dopamine agonists (pramipexole is the most likely culprit in this patient) > amantadine > levodopa > MAO-B inhibitors. Anticholinergics are high risk.\n2. **PD dementia progression**: Hallucinations with cognitive decline suggest evolving Lewy body spectrum disease\n3. **Delirium**: UTI, metabolic derangement, infection — always rule out acute causes\n4. **REM sleep behavior disorder**: May overlap with hypnagogic/hypnopompic hallucinations\n\n**Workup**:\n- CBC, CMP, urinalysis — rule out delirium\n- Medication reconciliation — timing of hallucinations relative to doses\n- Formal neuropsychological testing — distinguish PD-MCI from PD dementia\n- Ophthalmologic exam — rule out Charles Bonnet syndrome (visual loss-related hallucinations)\n\n**Management Strategy (Stepwise Medication Adjustment)**:\n1. **First**: Reduce or eliminate dopamine agonist (pramipexole) — this is the most common culprit and should be tapered first\n2. **Second**: Reduce rasagiline if hallucinations persist\n3. **Third**: If carbidopa-levodopa must be maintained for motor function, add **pimavanserin** (Nuplazid) — a selective serotonin inverse agonist (5-HT2A) FDA-approved specifically for PD psychosis. Does not worsen motor symptoms.\n4. **Alternative**: Low-dose quetiapine (12.5-50 mg at bedtime) — commonly used off-label. Avoid all other typical and atypical antipsychotics (haloperidol, olanzapine, risperidone) as they block D2 receptors and catastrophically worsen parkinsonism.\n5. **If dementia is confirmed**: Consider cholinesterase inhibitor (rivastigmine — the only one FDA-approved for PD dementia) which may also reduce hallucinations.',
